        "synthesis": {
          "title": "Cracks in the Islamic Republic, v2 — adding clergy, students, prisons, pharma, cyber (17 Apr – 1 May 2026)",
          "summary": "Six new cracks crystallise alongside the four previously documented cracks. (5) Clerical defection: Mowlawi Abdolhamid — Iran's most-followed Sunni Friday-prayer leader — said publicly on 21 Apr that the country's air defence is broken and that the only path is a 'fair agreement' plus 'accountability for extremists who brought the country to ruin'. The regime answered by sealing the Makki Mosque-affiliated Azizi institute and freezing his associates' bank accounts. Former president Hassan Rouhani separately demanded a 'great change' and openly mocked attempts to grant '1% of demands while forgetting 99%' — an establishment-pragmatist break that contradicts the IRGC line. (6) Student strikes: Sharif University of Technology students started a class boycott from 25 Apr over the continued detention of Parnian Khodabakhshi; Beheshti University and others showed parallel disciplinary tension. (7) Prison resistance escalating: 'Tuesdays of No to Executions' hunger strike entered its 117th, then 118th week across 56 prisons, with female inmates joining despite explicit threats; 7 political prisoners were violently transferred from Evin to Qezel-Hesar Ward 3, which an Iran HRS exposé described as a 'silent execution chamber'. (8) Pharma collapse: drug prices up to 380% above pre-war levels; Persian financial press warning of a generalised drug crisis within two months; the parliament Health Commission's spokesperson explicitly warned of an imminent morphine shortage. (9) Cyber pressure on the bank-IRGC link: hackers claiming Israeli alignment said they destroyed data inside the IRGC-affiliated Bank Sepah, while Iran's Central Bank publicly denied 'the banking network surrendered'. (10) Kurdish escalation: a new wave of mass arrests across Kurdish-majority cities, with Western analysts (Jamestown) openly war-gaming a Kurdish capture of territory. Together with the four prior cracks (command vacuum, currency collapse, labour unrest, infrastructure & repression squeeze), the regime now faces simultaneous pressure on its religious legitimacy, its medical capacity, its banking integrity, and three large ethnic frontiers (Baluchistan, Kurdistan, Tehran-bourgeois students) — while official-mouthpiece denials about Mojtaba's health and the central bank's invulnerability are themselves diagnostic of strain.",
